 "raw_reply": "{\"item\": \"S442-017\",\n \"clauses\": {\n  \"compare\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F05\", \"template\": \"Look at the fraction models. Compare \\\\(\\\\frac{{{num}}}{{{den}}}\\\\) and \\\\(\\\\frac{{{b_num}}}{{{b_den}}}\\\\). Which sign makes the statement true: \\\\(\\\\frac{{{num}}}{{{den}}}\\\\) ___ \\\\(\\\\frac{{{b_num}}}{{{b_den}}}\\\\)?\", \"reasoning\": \"F05 contains the task word Compare in its question_text. Its num and b_num slots are fixed at 1 and its den and b_den slots draw 2 and 4, distinct in every render, so it prints the pair 1/2 and 1/4 and asks for a comparison sign.\"},\n  \"half\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F05\", \"template\": \"Look at the fraction models. Compare \\\\(\\\\frac{{{num}}}{{{den}}}\\\\) and \\\\(\\\\frac{{{b_num}}}{{{b_den}}}\\\\). Which sign makes the statement true: \\\\(\\\\frac{{{num}}}{{{den}}}\\\\) ___ \\\\(\\\\frac{{{b_num}}}{{{b_den}}}\\\\)?\", \"reasoning\": \"F05 prints one half as 1 over 2 in its question_text, since den can be 2 from the g1_pairs denominators with num fixed at 1.\"},\n  \"quarter\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F05\", \"template\": \"Look at the fraction models. Compare \\\\(\\\\frac{{{num}}}{{{den}}}\\\\) and \\\\(\\\\frac{{{b_num}}}{{{b_den}}}\\\\). Which sign makes the statement true: \\\\(\\\\frac{{{num}}}{{{den}}}\\\\) ___ \\\\(\\\\frac{{{b_num}}}{{{b_den}}}\\\\)?\", \"reasoning\": \"F05 prints one quarter as 1 over 4 in its question_text, since den or b_den can be 4 from the g1_pairs denominators with num fixed at 1.\"},\n  \"models\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F04\", \"template\": \"Look at the fraction models for \\\\(\\\\frac{{{num}}}{{{den}}}\\\\) and \\\\(\\\\frac{{{b_num}}}{{{b_den}}}\\\\). Which comparison sign is correct: \\\\(\\\\frac{{{num}}}{{{den}}}\\\\) ___ \\\\(\\\\frac{{{b_num}}}{{{b_den}}}\\\\)?\", \"reasoning\": \"F04 names fraction models as the thing the learner looks at, so the representation clause is named in its question_text.\"}\n },\n \"failing_families\": [\"F08\", \"F09\"],\n \"failing_templates\": [\"When {den_lbl}s are the same, the {frac_lbl} with the bigger {num_lbl} is larger.\", \"{num} vs {other_num}: the larger top number gives the larger {frac_lbl}.\"],\n \"overall\": \"FAIL\",\n \"reasoning\": \"F09 prints num and other_num from the numerator domain, which is fixed at 1 in every render (repeats a generated value, printed_runs 1, and b_num members [1]). Every problem therefore shows 1 vs 1 in its hint, and the statement that the larger top number gives the larger fraction compares nothing and cannot be applied to the pair 1/2 and 1/4. Its general claim is also not true for fraction pairs with unequal bottom numbers, so the hint teaches a false rule. F08 states a rule about comparing top numbers when bottom numbers are the same. That is a numerator-comparison rule outside what this lc names (compare using models), and its condition never holds for this lc's pairs, since den and b_den are distinct in every render. F07 is accepted: it only asks the learner to check whether the bottom numbers are equal, states no false rule, and its slots print 1, 2 and 4 from the generator's own values. F01 (bottom number), F02 (fraction) and F03 (top number) are gate words that use no NOT_YET_KNOWN terms. F04, F05 and F06 show only the 1/2 and 1/4 pair with sign choices, and F10 and F12 offer the signs < and > with the correct answer among them. F11 offers option keys A to D only. The vocabulary in the families is limited to cumulative_vocab words and the gate words, with no NOT_YET_KNOWN terms.\"}",
